one-use information is additive across every finite tensor power; hence its regularized information value is the same one-use maximum. This proves additivity for the information expression itself. [F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 For any input state $\\rho$ and positive integer $m$, exact spectral type reduction gives a flat state $\\tau_t$ on an $m$-use input subspace such that $$f_{\\mathcal N^{\\otimes m}}(\\tau_t)\\ge m f_{\\mathcal N}(\\rho)-2d\\ln(m+1),\\qquad d=\\dim A.$$ Apply the flat-state construction to that fixed block channel. For fixed $m$, both its Weyl alphabet and its receiver output dimension are finite, so the HSW theorem applies before any limit in $m$ is taken. The shared resource is the flat type-subspace maximally entangled state for each outer block. Padding fewer than $m$ unused original-channel inputs with a fixed state gives codes for all sufficiently large lengths and no asymptotic rate loss. Therefore $$C_E(\\mathcal N)\\ge f_{\\mathcal N}(\\rho)-\\frac{2d\\ln(m+1)}m.$$ Let $m\\to\\infty$ and maximize over $\\rho$. This proves the general direct bound with precisely the stated unlimited finite-shared-entanglement model. No unrestricted quantum-input HSW single-letter theorem and no inference from Schumacher compression alone is used. [F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, step 1.2, given, algebra, construct]\n\n3.1 Consider any length-$n$ stated EA code with uniform message register $M$, receiver's prior share $S$, channel input $A^n$, and receiver output $B^nS$. Before transmission its joint state is $$\\omega_{MA^nS}=\\frac1K\\sum_m|m\\rangle\\langle m|\\otimes(\\mathcal E_m\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_S)(\\sigma).$$ The marginal $MS$ is the product of the uniform message and the original share: each encoder is trace-preserving and hence cannot change $S$. In particular $I(M:S)=0$. Let $\\bar\\rho_{A^n}$ be its average input. By the extension lemma, the $MSA^n$ state is obtained by a channel on the reference of a purification of $\\bar\\rho_{A^n}$. After transmission, quantum-mutual-information data processing gives $$I(MS:B^n)\\le I(R:B^n)_{(\\operatorname{id}\\otimes\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})(\\psi_{\\bar\\rho})}.$$ Expanding the entropy definitions gives the exact identity $$I(M:B^nS)=I(MS:B^n)-I(S:B^n)+I(M:S).$$ The last term is zero, and $I(S:B^n)\\ge0$ by subadditivity. Therefore $$I(M:B^nS)\\le I(R:B^n)\\le F(\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})=nF(\\mathcal N).$$ The equality uses the stated optimized-information additivity lemma, whose proof follows local-channel mutual-information data processing and product-state entropy additivity. Bob's POVM and the Holevo bound imply $I(M;\\widehat M)\\le I(M:B^nS)$. Fano then gives $$(1-e_n)\\ln K_n\\le nF(\\mathcal N)+h(e_n).$$ Singleton message sets are trivial. Divide by $n$ and let average error tend to zero to bound every achievable rate by $F(\\mathcal N)$, without restricting the shared state, its dimension, or the message-dependent CPTP encoders. Together with the general direct bound, purification identity and attained maximum, this proves the entire retained operational formula. Finally applying the identity $C_E=F$ to both channels and their product, and using the stated proved additivity of $F$, proves the capacity additivity assertion as well. [F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, step 2.1, given, algebra, construct] ∎"
